{
 "id": "lift-every-voice",
 "version": 1,
 "reviewed": true,
 "title": "Lift Every Voice and Sing",
 "description": "James Weldon Johnson's poem to his brother J. Rosamond Johnson's music of 1900, long called the Black national anthem, for 2 to 4 singers: the first verse, broad and stately, a breath at every line of the poem. The parts are built on Rosamond Johnson's own four voices from the first printing, smoothed for beginners; where he has every voice sing \"Sing a song full of the faith\" in octaves, the alto, tenor and bass hold his organ's chords under the tune.",
 "language": "en",
 "difficulty": "intermediate",
 "genre": [
  "hymn",
  "american"
 ],
 "style": "straight",
 "tempo": 60,
 "key": "G",
 "timeSignature": "6/8",
 "pickupBeats": 1.0,
 "credits": {
  "words": "James Weldon Johnson (1900)",
  "tune": "J. Rosamond Johnson (1900)",
  "arrangement": "J. Rosamond Johnson's own four parts (1900), adapted by Stackapella"
 },
 "licence": {
  "words": "public domain: published 1900; James Weldon Johnson died in 1938",
  "tune": "public domain: published 1900; J. Rosamond Johnson died in 1954",
  "arrangement": "J. Rosamond Johnson's four voice parts as Jos. W. Stern & Co. first printed them (public domain: published 1900), a semitone lower: his tune, sung one note a syllable (two slurred notes left out) with the fermata on \"us;\" written out as a held note and a bar's pause; his alto, tenor and bass, with 11 alto, 19 tenor and 14 bass notes changed (most in the three pickups, where his four voices rise together in octaves and fifths) so the parts avoid parallel octaves, a doubled leading tone and awkward leaps and keep the chord's third for two and three singers, and one note to a syllable where he slurs two (four places); in bars 16 to 22, where his voices sing the tune in octaves, the alto, tenor and bass are Stackapella's, on his organ part's harmony; breaths written in for beginners",
  "recordings": "synthesized guides",
  "checked": null,
  "source": "J. Rosamond Johnson's own setting, \"Lift Every Voice and Sing\" (\"National Hymn for the Colored People of America\", words by J. W. Johnson, music by Rosamond Johnson; New York: Jos. W. Stern & Co., 1900, plate 3071-3), its two vocal staves (soprano and alto, tenor and bass) under the words of verse 1, in A-flat, written here a semitone lower in G. Read from the British Library's copy (stamped 3 Nov. 1900) as reproduced, all four pages, in Chris Fenner, \"Lift every voice and sing\", Hymnology Archive (2019), figure 1: hymnologyarchive.com/lift-every-voice-and-sing. Checked against the Edward B. Marks Music Co. \"Mixed Quartette\" printing (plate 8088-3, \"Copyright MCM by Jos. W. Stern & Co.\", renewed 1927, assigned to Marks 1928; signed by J. Rosamond Johnson), Beinecke Rare Book and Manuscript Library, Yale University, James Weldon Johnson Memorial Collection, JWJ -V2 J632 L61d: collections.library.yale.edu/catalog/2053069 (the tune the same note for note)"
 },
